# Feature: the TotalRate keyword on a reaction rule.
# Tests:   TotalRate says the rate law IS the propensity, rather than the
#          rate per set of reactants.  So the rule fires at a fixed rate no
#          matter how many reactants are present, and its reactant decays in
#          a straight line instead of an exponential.  That is the whole
#          content of the keyword, and it is what every arm here measures.
#
#          RM refuses TotalRate models at load, because BioNetGen does not
#          implement the keyword for network simulations and NFsim's reading
#          of it differs from RM's on rules whose reactant pattern has
#          interchangeable components (see COVERAGE.md).  The harness runs
#          this model with --ignore-unsupported so the propensity path stays
#          covered.  Every arm below deliberately avoids interchangeable
#          components, which is exactly the region where RM and NFsim have
#          one answer between them.
#
# Oracle:  NFsim, plus a closed form per arm.
#            RA  constant rate kd, so Aoff falls in a straight line and
#                reaches zero at t = 60/0.5 = 120, then stays there
#            RB  constant rate kf on a two-reactant rule: Bfree falls in a
#                straight line at kf, unaffected by either reactant's count
#            RH  the rate law is a function of an observable, so the
#                propensity tracks it: dH/dt = -kh*H, an exponential
#          The three shapes together separate TotalRate from mass action in
#          both directions: RA/RB are linear where mass action is curved, and
#          RH is exponential where mass action would be hyperbolic.
#
# Tier:    network-free.
# Expected (t_end = 200):
#            Aoff   60 -> 0 by t = 120 (straight line, slope -0.5)
#            Bfree  200 -> 100         (straight line, slope -0.5)
#            Hoff   200 -> 200*exp(-kh*200) = 27.1
#          Under mass action instead of TotalRate every arm is orders of
#          magnitude faster: RB alone would run at kf*200*200 = 20000.
# Regression: the model this replaces fired 4 events over its whole run and
#          could not tell TotalRate from anything else.  Its header also
#          stated the semantics wrongly ("binding rate is exactly k*[A]*[B]")
#          — TotalRate removes the counts entirely, it does not merely
#          suppress the site multiplicity.
# invariant: conserved Atot = 60
# invariant: nonneg

begin model
begin parameters
    kd   0.5
    kf   0.5
    kh   1.0e-2
end parameters

begin molecule types
    A(s~0~1)
    B(b)
    Cc(b)
    H(s~0~1)
end molecule types

begin seed species
    A(s~0)   60
    B(b)     200
    Cc(b)    200
    H(s~0)   200
end seed species

begin observables
    Molecules  Aoff   A(s~0)
    Molecules  Atot   A()
    Molecules  Bfree  B(b)
    Molecules  Hoff   H(s~0)
end observables

begin functions
    hf() = kh*Hoff
end functions

begin reaction rules
    # Constant rate, and the reactant runs out: the propensity has to stop
    # when it does.
    RA: A(s~0) -> A(s~1)                kd TotalRate
    # Two reactants, neither count entering the rate.
    RB: B(b) + Cc(b) -> B(b!1).Cc(b!1)  kf TotalRate
    # The rate law moves during the run, and is still the whole propensity.
    RH: H(s~0) -> H(s~1)                hf() TotalRate
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>200,n_steps=>100})
end actions
